course: dictionary
lesson: 1657
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word chips is a good example. Read the whole sentence first, then ask which meaning fits. «We had fish and chips by the sea.» Here chips are the hot fried potatoes, because fish and chips is a meal.
explain: «The computer stopped working because a chip inside it had broken.» Here chip means a tiny electronic part, not a piece of potato. Words like computer and working point to the right meaning. Always try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different countries. «British "chips" are American "fries"; British "crisps" are American "chips".» A reader in the USA and a reader in the UK might picture different foods. Where the writer lives can be a clue too.
explain: Slang is another kind of context. «Popped his clogs», «brown bread» and «cashed in his chips» all mean died, but they are jokes, not plain words. When a phrase seems odd, ask whether its meaning fits the whole sentence. If it still does not, check a dictionary.
fact: Chips is a noun, and it is the plural of chip.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, «chips» can mean long pieces of fried potato, which are called «fries» in the USA.
fact: In the USA and Canada, «chips» are thin, crisp slices of fried potato sold in packets; in the UK and Ireland these are called «crisps».
fact: «Chips» can also mean tiny electronic parts inside computers and phones, such as «microchips».
fact: «When the chips are down» means when things are hard. You cannot always work out an idiom from its separate words, so check its meaning in context.
exercise: «We ordered fish and chips at the seaside.» In this sentence, what are chips? | Tiny parts inside a computer | *Long pieces of fried potato | Small broken pieces of wood | Small plastic tokens for a board game
exercise: «The broken chip in the phone meant the screen would not light up.» What does chip mean here? | A small broken piece of stone | *A tiny electronic part inside the phone | A plastic counter used in games | A slice of potato
exercise: «The pet had a microchip under its skin, so it could be returned if it was lost.» Why does the pet have a microchip? | To make its fur shine | To help it run faster | To stop it from getting hungry | *So it can be found and returned if it is lost
test: «He saw a chip of paint on the old door.» What does chip mean here? | A plastic game token | *A small piece broken off | A tiny computer part | A long fried potato
test: «When the chips are down, friends help each other.» What does the phrase mean? | *When things are hard | When it is time for lunch | When a game has ended | When a computer shuts down
exam: «An SSD stores data on chips with no moving parts.» What does chips mean here? | Long fried potatoes | *Tiny electronic parts | Pieces of wood | Small plastic tokens
